  "summary": "An opinion piece by criminologist Steven Keener urging Richmond, VA to turn off its Flock Safety ALPR cameras, citing Fourth Amendment concerns, racially disparate camera placement, documented misuse including ATF access for immigration and searches tied to No Kings protests, and weak compliance with Virginia's HB2724. The author notes Richmond City Council recently voted to continue funding ALPRs and points to Staunton and Charlottesville as cities that canceled their Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives (ATF) improperly accessed collected images from Flock cameras in Richmond for an immigration investigation in March, and a Richmod police officer recently shared Flock data with a federal agent.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "In Richmond, the ALPR database was searched in association with the No Kings protests in 2025",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Of the 12 most surveilled census tracts in Richmond, 11 are majority non-white",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "13% of agencies admitted to giving out-of-state entities continuous access to ALPR data; 6% have granted the federal government continuous access",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "cities like Staunton and Charlottesville have cancelled their Flock contracts altogether after recognizing the risks. Richmond should follow their lead.",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
    }
  ],
